<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  One place that decides what "covered" means, shared by every suite, so two of them can never measure
  different things and then be compared anyway. Read by dotnet-coverage, which scripts/Run-Tests.ps1 wraps
  each suite in.

  **THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO <Attributes> BLOCK, AND THE REASON IS CompilerGeneratedAttribute.** Excluding
  it sounds like housekeeping - skip what the compiler wrote rather than what anybody typed - and it is
  nothing of the sort. An async method's body compiles into MoveNext on a [CompilerGenerated] state
  machine, and a lambda into a [CompilerGenerated] closure, so excluding the attribute DELETES THE BODY OF
  EVERY ASYNC METHOD AND EVERY LAMBDA from the report. MEASURED on one run of the engine suite, lines
  counted in Cascade.Core: 4,613 with no exclusions, 4,232 excluding CompilerGenerated, 4,613 excluding
  DebuggerHidden. That is 381 lines, 8% of the assembly, and it is not spread evenly - it falls on the
  async-heavy code, which is the update and network path. GitHubReleaseSource.cs read 12 of 12 lines, a
  perfect score, where the truth is 70 of 77; GitCredentialToken.cs read 13 of 17 where the truth is 25 of
  64. The error flatters exactly the files with the least testing, which is the one thing the figure exists
  to find.

  Nothing is lost by having no block: [ExcludeFromCodeCoverage] is honoured anyway (verified - UiShots,
  DocShots, RenderShots and ScrollBench are all absent from the report), and excluding DebuggerHidden was
  measured to change nothing at all.

  THE TOOL IS dotnet-coverage RATHER THAN THE ONE IN THE TEST SDK for a different reason: the UI suite
  launches the app as its own process, this file used to say CollectFromChildProcesses=false, and the SDK's
  collector will not follow a child anyway. That is why a third of the app's code read as untested for as
  long as there has been a coverage number here.

  ModulePaths names the two product assemblies explicitly. Left to itself a collector reports on whatever
  it finds beside the test assembly, which pads the figure with the tests' own lines - and a coverage
  number that includes the test code measures nothing.

  **THE MODULE PATTERNS MUST BE LOWERCASE.** The path is lowercased before it is matched, so the obvious
  `.*\\Cascade\.dll$` matches NOTHING and the whole filter silently does nothing at all - which is how this
  file sat here excluding neither assembly. Report-Coverage.ps1 drops anything named `*Tests` as well, so a
  filter that stops matching again cannot quietly put the test code back into the figure.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <DataCollectionRunSettings>
    <DataCollectors>
      <DataCollector friendlyName="Code Coverage">
        <Configuration>
          <Format>cobertura</Format>
          <CodeCoverage>
            <ModulePaths>
              <Include>
                <ModulePath>.*\\cascade\.dll$</ModulePath>
                <ModulePath>.*\\cascade\.core\.dll$</ModulePath>
              </Include>
            </ModulePaths>
          </CodeCoverage>
        </Configuration>
      </DataCollector>
    </DataCollectors>
  </DataCollectionRunSettings>
</RunSettings>
